1
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:15,000
Verified boot is a critical security feature in Android that ensures the integrity of the system during

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,000
the boot process.

3
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:21,720
It establishes a chain of trust from the hardware to the operating system, preventing the execution

4
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:27,240
of unauthorized code and protecting against malicious attacks that attempt to compromise the system's

5
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:28,080
integrity.

6
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:34,240
At its core, verified boot relies on cryptographic verification to ensure that each stage of the boot

7
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:37,080
process is genuine and has not been tampered with.

8
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:42,400
The process begins with a hardware rooted trust anchor, typically a read only memory containing the

9
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:43,560
initial bootloader.

10
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:49,480
This bootloader is digitally signed by the device manufacturer, and its authenticity can be verified

11
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:52,200
using a public key embedded in the hardware.

12
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:58,560
When the device is powered on, the initial boot loader verifies the signature of the next stage bootloader,

13
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:02,040
which is responsible for loading the operating system kernel.

14
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:07,500
If the signature is valid, the bootloader proceeds to load and execute the kernel.

15
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:14,100
If the signature verification fails at any point, the boot process is halted, preventing the execution

16
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:16,060
of potentially malicious code.

17
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:22,260
The kernel in turn, verifies the signatures of the system partitions which contain the core operating

18
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:24,500
system files and applications.

19
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:28,700
This ensures that the entire system has not been modified or corrupted.

20
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:34,740
This verification process continues throughout the boot chain, with each component verifying the authenticity

21
00:01:34,740 --> 00:01:35,540
of the next.

22
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:41,100
This creates a chain of trust that extends from the hardware to the operating system, ensuring that

23
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:43,380
only authorized code is executed.

24
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:48,820
One of the key benefits of verified boot is its ability to protect against rollback attacks.

25
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:54,500
Rollback attacks involve reverting the system to an earlier, vulnerable version of the operating system

26
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:57,180
in order to exploit known security flaws.

27
00:01:57,620 --> 00:02:03,220
Verified boot prevents rollback attacks by requiring each version of the operating system to be cryptographically

28
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:03,740
signed.

29
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:08,820
The device maintains a record of the highest version of the operating system that has been verified,

30
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:11,760
and will refuse to boot any version that is older than this.

31
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:17,080
This effectively prevents attackers from downgrading the system to a vulnerable state.

32
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:21,960
Furthermore, verified boot can detect tampering with the system partitions.

33
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:28,000
If an attacker attempts to modify the system partitions, the verification process will fail, preventing

34
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,600
the system from booting.

35
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:36,440
This ensures that the device is always running a genuine and unmodified version of the operating system.

36
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:37,680
In summary.

37
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:43,120
Verified boot plays a vital role in safeguarding the integrity of the Android system, ensuring that

38
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:48,600
only authorized code is executed, preventing rollback attacks, and detecting tampering with system

39
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:49,400
partitions.

40
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:54,760
It forms a fundamental part of the Android security architecture, and is essential for maintaining

41
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:57,800
the security and trustworthiness of Android devices.

42
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:04,080
It's a sophisticated mechanism, but with simple aim of verifying the authenticity of each component.

